description:
Decode the first two layers of a captured frame (lesson 4.02).

```cpp
struct Ipv4
{
    std::uint8_t headerLength;     // in bytes
    std::uint16_t totalLength;
    std::uint16_t id;
    bool dontFragment;
    bool moreFragments;
    std::uint16_t fragmentOffset;  // in bytes
    std::uint8_t ttl;
    std::uint8_t protocol;
    std::uint32_t source;
    std::uint32_t destination;
    bool checksumOk;
};

std::string macToString(const std::vector<std::uint8_t>& frame, std::size_t offset);
std::string ipToString(std::uint32_t ip);
std::optional<std::uint16_t> etherType(const std::vector<std::uint8_t>& frame, std::size_t& payloadOffset);
std::optional<Ipv4> parseIpv4(const std::vector<std::uint8_t>& packet);
```

- **macToString** writes the six bytes starting at `offset` as lower-case hex pairs separated by colons (`3c:22:fb:12:9a:01`), or returns an empty string if they do not fit.
- **ipToString** writes dotted decimal: `0xC0A80001` is `192.168.0.1`.
- **etherType** returns the EtherType at bytes 12-13 and sets `payloadOffset` to 14. If it is a VLAN tag (`0x8100`), the real EtherType is at bytes 16-17 and the payload starts at 18. It fails if the frame is too short for what it needs.
- **parseIpv4** decodes an IPv4 header at the start of `packet`. It fails if the packet is shorter than 20 bytes, the version is not 4, the header length is under 20 or longer than the packet, or the total length is less than the header length or more than the packet's size (a packet may be longer than its total length: that is Ethernet padding). The fragment offset is converted to bytes. `checksumOk` says whether the Internet checksum over the header comes out as 0; a bad checksum does not make the parse fail.

